[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Interfaces, Abstract Contracts, and Libraries in Solidity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Solidity provides powerful architectural patterns to modularize code, enforce standards, and save gas. This tutorial covers interfaces, abstract contracts, and libraries, explaining when and how to use each for robust smart contract development."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Interfaces"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interfaces are similar to abstract contracts, but they cannot have any functions implemented. They define a standard structure that other contracts must follow. Key rules for interfaces include: they cannot inherit from other smart contracts (only other interfaces), they cannot declare state variables, and they cannot define constructors."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"interface IERC20 {
    function totalSupply() external view returns (uint256);
    function balanceOf(address account) external view returns (uint256);
    function transfer(address recipient, uint256 amount) external returns (bool);
    event Transfer(address indexed from, address indexed to, uint256 value);
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Abstract Contracts"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Abstract contracts are base contracts that contain at least one function without implementation. They are marked with the 'abstract' keyword. They are useful when you want to share common state variables and logic among multiple child contracts while forcing children to implement specific missing functionality."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"abstract contract Authorizable {
    address public owner;

    constructor() {
        owner = msg.sender;
    }

    modifier onlyOwner() {
        require(msg.sender == owner, "Not authorized");
        _;
    }

    function executeAction() external virtual onlyOwner;
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Libraries"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Libraries are similar to contracts, but they are deployed only once at a specific address, and their code is reused using delegatecall. They cannot have state variables, cannot inherit, and cannot receive Ether. They are ideal for writing reusable helper functions and math operations."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"library SafeMath {
    function add(uint256 a, uint256 b) internal pure returns (uint256) {
        uint256 c = a + b;
        require(c >= a, "SafeMath: addition overflow");
        return c;
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary of Best Practices"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Interfaces when interacting with external contracts or defining strict standards like ERC-20."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Abstract Contracts when you want to share code (state and functions) via inheritance."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Libraries for stateless utility functions that help keep your main contracts lightweight and secure."}]
